    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d129">

                <pb facs="0189" n="1023" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1023"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.119/581</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-23T20:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1918, 8 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7014. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 238. Embassy’s unnumbered telegram dated March 13, 11 a.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d129-f1"/> You may advise Owen, Stockholm, that it is not practical
                    for time being to operate with a white list. The next revised edition of the
                    United States enemy trading list, now in printers’ hands, will contain names of
                    firms with whom it is illegal to trade. It follows that trading with all firms
                    not on that list will be legal.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
